To build an enterprise-grade Google Cloud Platform (GCP) environment from scratch, you need a structured Landing Zone. Below is the end-to-end architectural blueprint covering hierarchy, networking, workloads, databases, DevOps, Disaster Recovery (DR), and security.

1. Resource Hierarchy & Project Design

Instead of putting everything into a single project, use GCP’s hierarchical model to isolate environments, billing, and IAM permissions.
• Organization Node: The root node tied to your corporate domain (Cloud Identity / Google Workspace).
• Folders: Group projects by department or environment (e.g., Shared-Infra, Production, Non-Prod).
• Projects (The Security & Billing Boundary):
	• prj-shared-vpc-host: Owns the central network (VPCs, subnets, firewalls, Cloud NAT, Interconnect).
	• prj-shared-devops: Hosts Artifact Registry, CI/CD runners, and Terraform state buckets.
	• prj-prod-app / prj-nonprod-app: Attached as Service Projects to the Shared VPC. Compute (GKE, VMs) and DBs live here.
	• prj-security-logs: Centralized destination for Cloud Audit Logs and VPC Flow Logs.

2. Networking, Traffic Flow & Firewalls

Unlike AWS, GCP VPCs are global resources, and subnets are regional.

Shared VPC & Subnetting

Create a Shared VPC in the Host project. Divide subnets by tier and region (e.g., asia-south1 Mumbai for primary in India, asia-south2 Delhi for DR):
• snet-proxy-only: Required for Regional External/Internal Application Load Balancers.
• snet-gke-nodes: For GKE worker nodes (with secondary IP ranges for Pods and Services).
• snet-monolith-vms: For Compute Engine VMs running legacy/monolith apps.
• snet-data: For managed database private endpoints.

NACLs, Security Groups, & Firewalls in GCP

GCP does not use traditional AWS-style Subnet NACLs or instance-attached Security Groups. Instead, it uses stateful, distributed software-defined firewalls at the VPC/Folder level:
1. VPC Firewall Rules: Applied at the VPC level. You target specific VMs/GKE nodes using Service Accounts (recommended for production) or Network Tags. Example: Allow TCP 8080 from sa-apigee to sa-gke-nodes.
2. Hierarchical Firewall Policies: Applied at the Organization or Folder level to enforce global guardrails (e.g., blocking SSH/RDP from 0.0.0.0/0 across all projects).
3. Cloud Armor: Acts as your Web Application Firewall (WAF) and DDoS protection at the Global Load Balancer edge before traffic enters your VPC.

End-to-End Traffic Flow

• North-South (External to Internal):
Client → Cloud DNS → Global External Load Balancer + Cloud Armor → Apigee X (API Gateway) → Internal Load Balancer (ILB) → GKE Ingress / Monolith Managed Instance Group (MIG) → Database.
• East-West (Service-to-Service):
	• For I90 Framework / Microservices on GKE: Use Cloud Service Mesh (Anthos Service Mesh) for mTLS encryption, traffic splitting, and observability between pods.
	• For Monolith to GKE: Route via an Internal Application Load Balancer using Private Google Access / Internal DNS.

3. Compute, API Management & Storage


Applications (Monolith & GKE)

• Monolith: Deploy on Compute Engine Managed Instance Groups (MIGs) across multiple zones for auto-healing and auto-scaling. Bake golden images using Packer.
• Microservices (I90 Framework): Deploy on GKE Autopilot or GKE Standard (Private Cluster). If your I90 framework relies on specific middleware or custom runtime agents, use GKE Standard with Workload Identity enabled so pods authenticate to GCP services without static JSON keys.

API Management (Apigee X)

• Provision Apigee X using VPC Peering (Private Service Access) so API traffic stays entirely on Google’s private backbone.
• Configure Apigee as the North-South gateway to handle OAuth2/JWT validation, rate-limiting, spike arrest, and routing to your backend GKE services or Monolith.

Utilizing Your Storage System

• Cloud Storage (GCS): For unstructured object storage (assets, backups, data lake). Use Autoclass to automatically transition cold data to Nearline/Coldline/Archive tiers.
• Hybrid Storage (On-Prem SAN/NAS Integration): If you must integrate an existing on-prem storage appliance:
	• Use Storage Transfer Service (with on-prem agents) for automated data syncs to GCS.
	• For low-latency POSIX file shares needed by your Monolith VMs, use Google Cloud NetApp Volumes or Filestore to replicate/replace on-prem NAS shares.

4. Database Configuration (Cloud Spanner & Redis)


Cloud Spanner

• Instance Sizing: Start with granular Processing Units (e.g., 100–500 PUs) rather than full nodes to optimize cost, scaling up automatically as traffic grows.
• Schema Best Practices: Avoid monotonically increasing primary keys (like auto-incrementing integers or timestamps) to prevent write hotspots. Use UUIDv4, Bit-Reversed Sequences, or composite keys.
• Connectivity: Connect from GKE/VMs via Private Service Connect (PSC) to keep traffic off the public internet.

Redis Sharding (Memorystore)

• Standard Memorystore for Redis does not support horizontal sharding. To achieve sharding and high availability:
	• Deploy Memorystore for Redis Cluster (supports automatic data sharding across multiple shards, zero-downtime scaling, and cross-zone replication).
	• Ensure your application client (e.g., Lettuce/Jedis for Java, ioredis for Node) is Cluster-aware to handle MOVED redirections and slot mapping.

5. Hybrid Connectivity & Disaster Recovery (DR)


On-Premises Communication

• Dedicated or Partner Interconnect: Best for high-throughput, low-latency production traffic between your on-prem data center and GCP (available via major Indian telecom partners in Mumbai/Chennai).
• HA VPN: Use two Cloud VPN tunnels with Cloud Router (BGP) for dynamic route exchange as a backup to Interconnect, or as a primary link if bandwidth needs are under 3 Gbps.
• Private Google Access for On-Premises: Allows your on-prem servers to reach Google APIs (Spanner, GCS) privately over the VPN/Interconnect without public IPs.

Disaster Recovery (DR) Strategy

• RPO/RTO Tiers:
	• Database: Use a Multi-Region Spanner configuration (e.g., nam-eur-asia or regional dual-region setups) for near-zero RPO, or automated cross-region backups/exports for standard regional Spanner. Configure Memorystore Redis persistence (RDB snapshots) exported to a multi-region GCS bucket.
	• Compute: Maintain warm/pilot-light GKE clusters and MIG templates in a secondary region (e.g., Primary: asia-south1 Mumbai, DR: asia-south2 Delhi). Use Global Load Balancers to automatically shift traffic to the healthy region during an outage.

6. DevOps & CI/CD (GitHub Actions / GitLab CI)

1. Infrastructure as Code (IaC): Manage the entire landing zone (Projects, VPCs, Firewalls, Spanner, GKE) using Terraform modularized by environment.
2. Keyless Authentication (OIDC): Never store GCP Service Account JSON keys in GitHub or GitLab secrets. Configure Workload Identity Federation so GitHub Actions or GitLab CI pipelines exchange short-lived OIDC tokens for temporary GCP IAM credentials.
3. Pipeline Flow:
	• CI: Build container → Scan image for CVEs (Artifact Analysis) → Push to Artifact Registry.
	• CD: Use Cloud Deploy or ArgoCD (GitOps) running inside GKE to pull the signed image and perform Canary or Blue/Green rollouts.

7. Monitoring, Observability & Security Standards


Observability Stack

• Cloud Monitoring & Logging: Collect GKE metrics, VM telemetry (via Ops Agent), and Spanner CPU/latency metrics. Set up SLO-based alerting policies routed to PagerDuty/Slack.
• Cloud Trace & Profiler: Instrument your I90/monolith apps with OpenTelemetry to trace request latency across Apigee → GKE → Spanner.

Security & Compliance Standards

• Organization Policies: Enforce constraints like iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation (blocks static keys), compute.vmExternalIpAccess (forces private IPs on VMs), and gcp.resourceLocations (restricts data residency to India/approved regions).
• VPC Service Controls (VPC-SC): Wrap your production projects (prj-prod-app, Spanner, GCS) in a VPC-SC perimeter to prevent data exfiltration, even if an IAM credential is compromised.
• Security Command Center (SCC): Enable SCC Premium to continuously scan for misconfigurations, public buckets, IAM anomalies, and compliance benchmarks (CIS, ISO 27001, SOC2, PCI-DSS).
• Secret Manager: Store all DB passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates in GCP Secret Manager with automatic rotation.
